.\" Manual page for cfspeed.
.\"
.\" Written against src/cli.c, which is the single authoritative parser: the
.\" flag list, the spelling and the value ranges below are transcribed from
.\" cli_usage() and cli_parse() rather than from a README, so the two cannot
.\" disagree without this file being the one that is wrong.
.TH CFSPEED 1 "2026-10-12" "cfspeed 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
cfspeed \- terminal speed test against Cloudflare's public speed endpoints
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B cfspeed
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B cfspeed
measures the quality of an internet connection against the public speed test
endpoints operated by Cloudflare at
.BR speed.cloudflare.com .
A single run reports three things: latency, download throughput and upload
throughput, each taken against whichever Cloudflare edge the machine's own DNS
and routing happen to reach.
.PP
Latency is sampled as round-trip time in both an idle window, before any
transfer saturates the link, and a loaded window, while a download is running.
The difference between the two is reported as bufferbloat: the queueing delay
the link adds under load. Alongside the medians,
.B cfspeed
reports jitter, packet loss and percentiles up to p99, a coefficient of
variation for throughput stability, and letter grades for the stability and
bufferbloat figures.
.PP
By default the run is presented in a full-screen terminal user interface drawn
with ANSI escape sequences, in a colour scheme derived from Tokyo Night. The
interface enables SGR mouse reporting, and the tab strip along the top is
mouse\-clickable as well as reachable from the keyboard. Five views exist:
.BR LIVE ,
.BR CHARTS ,
.BR DETAILS ,
.BR HISTORY
and
.BR HELP .
They are selected with the number keys 1 to 5, with
.B ?
for help, and with
.B Tab
and
.B "Shift\-Tab"
to step forwards and backwards. What each view draws is the subject of its own
source; this page documents the command line, not the contents of the views.
.PP
.B cfspeed
is also usable without a terminal, which is the point of most of its options.
.B \-\-json
prints a single JSON object on standard output and exits, and
.B \-\-quiet
suppresses the human-readable summary. The two headless outputs are produced
from the same measurement as the interface, not from a second implementation of
it, so a scripted number and an on-screen number come from one run.
.PP
A run is headless when
.B \-\-json
is given, because a JSON object cannot be produced from a full-screen interface.
The options that govern such a run are
.BR \-\-iterations ,
.B \-\-quiet
and
.BR \-\-out ;
they describe a run that starts, finishes and exits on its own, and they have
nothing to act on when the interface stays up between phases. A headless run is
never written to the history file, whatever
.B \-\-no-history
says.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-ping
Run the latency measurement only. The transfer phases are skipped, so the run
finishes quickly and consumes almost no bandwidth.
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-download
Run the download measurement only.
.TP
.BR \-u ", " \-\-upload
Run the upload measurement only.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Print one JSON object on standard output, then exit. Implies a headless run: a
JSON object cannot be produced from a full-screen interface. The object is
written with a single
.BR write (2)
and is followed by a newline, so a consumer reading a pipe sees it whole. Its
field names match the engine's own result structure. Units are not encoded in
the field names, so throughput is in Mbps, latency and bufferbloat are in
milliseconds, and loss and stability coefficient of variation are percentages.
Non-finite numbers, which a degenerate window can produce, are written as
.B null
rather than as a value JSON cannot spell.
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Suppress the human-readable summary. The summary goes to standard error and the
JSON object goes to standard output, so
.B \-\-quiet
lets a caller take the machine-readable output without the prose. It applies to
headless runs; the interface does not print that summary in the first place.
.TP
.BI \-\-iterations " N"
Run the test
.I N
times. The default is 1. The accepted range is 1 to 1000; a value outside it,
or a value that is not a whole number, is a usage error rather than a silently
clamped one. Each run after the first restarts the engine, so the iterations are
sequential rather than concurrent. This governs headless runs only; in the
interface a test is re\-run with
.BR r .
.TP
.BI \-\-duration " N"
Override the duration, in seconds, of each transfer phase. The default, written
as 0, means use the built\-in defaults of 10 seconds per phase. The accepted
range is 0 to 3600.
.B \-\-duration
is an error in ping mode: a ping run has no transfer phase, so the number would
have nothing to change. It is rejected rather than accepted and ignored.
.PP
The value is currently parsed and validated but not handed to the measurement
engine, so a transfer phase still runs for its built\-in length. The option is
described here as the parser accepts it, not as a guarantee about the length of
a run.
.TP
.BI \-\-out " \fIPATH\fR"
Also write the JSON summary to
.IR PATH .
The path must be at least one character and shorter than 512 characters
including the terminator. A path cut short by the buffer is refused, because
writing a result to a name the user never chose is worse than not writing one.
.PP
The file receives the same bytes as standard output, so the two can never
disagree: it is the same serialiser writing through a redirected stream rather
than a second copy of the document. Unlike standard output, the file is not
given a trailing newline. The file is written when the run finishes, which for
this option means a headless run; it is not written by the interface.
.TP
.B \-\-no-history
Do not persist this run to the on\-disk history file. The history belongs to the
interface, which is where a run is recorded on finish; a headless run never
reaches that code and is never recorded either way.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a usage summary and exit.
.PP
The three value\-taking options
.BR \-\-iterations ,
.B \-\-duration
and
.B \-\-out
accept both the separated form
.RB ( \-\-duration 15 )
and the attached form
.RB ( \-\-duration=15 ).
The attached form is available for long options only; every short option in this
program stands alone. A value\-taking option given with no value is treated as a
typo and ends the run, not as a request for the built\-in default.
.PP
An unrecognised argument is an error and ends the run. Silently ignoring a
misspelled flag is how a user comes to believe they capped a duration that they
did not cap.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The run completed,
.BR \-\-help
was requested,
.B \-\-version
was requested, or the interface was opened and then closed.
.TP
.B 1
The HTTP client, the engine, the terminal or the screen could not be
initialised.
.TP
.B 2
Either a usage error or a failed run, in a headless invocation.
.PP
A usage error is an unknown option, a missing or malformed value for a
value\-taking option, a value outside the documented range,
.B \-\-duration
used in ping mode, or an empty or over\-long
.B \-\-out
path.
.PP
The same status also covers two failures that are not usage errors, so that a
script can test one value rather than two: a headless run that produced no valid
result, and a
.B \-\-out
file that could not be written. Both are reported on standard error before the
run exits. Distinguish them by the message on that stream, or by reading the
.B valid
field of the JSON object when using
.BR \-\-json .
A run that fails from the interface does not exit at all: the failure is shown
and the interface stays up.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I cfspeed\-ca.pem
A CA bundle looked for next to the
.B cfspeed
executable, and required on Windows.
.PP
On Linux and macOS the system trust store is found without help and
.B cfspeed
uses it. A Windows
.BR libcurl (3)
built against GnuTLS or OpenSSL has no store of its own, so every request would
otherwise fail peer verification. On Windows,
.B cfspeed
therefore loads
.I cfspeed\-ca.pem
from the directory holding the executable. The file must be shipped alongside the
binary. It is not optional and there is no flag to skip it: turning verification
off would be a security regression in a tool whose entire job is trusting a
remote endpoint.
.TP
.I $HOME/cfspeed/history.json
Run history, as a JSON array with one object per line, oldest first, trimmed to
the newest 200 runs on save.
.PP
The base directory is
.I $HOME
on Linux and macOS, falling back to
.IR $XDG_DATA_HOME ,
and is
.I %LOCALAPPDATA%
on Windows, falling back to
.IR %USERPROFILE% .
If none of those environment variables is set the path is relative, so history
is written beside the binary rather than failing every save.
.B \-\-no-history
suppresses writing to this file for one run.
.SH EXAMPLES
Measure everything and watch it in the interface:
.PP
.RS
.EX
cfspeed
.EE
.RE
.PP
Measure everything without a terminal, taking the JSON:
.PP
.RS
.EX
cfspeed \-\-json
.EE
.RE
.PP
Check latency without spending any of the link's bandwidth:
.PP
.RS
.EX
cfspeed \-\-ping
.EE
.RE
.PP
Run the full test three times and write the last result to a file, keeping the
summary off the terminal:
.PP
.RS
.EX
cfspeed \-\-iterations 3 \-\-quiet \-\-out result.json
.EE
.RE
.PP
Request 30-second transfer phases on a download run. The value is accepted and
range\-checked, but the phase length is not yet applied, so the phase still runs
for its built\-in length:
.PP
.RS
.EX
cfspeed \-\-download \-\-duration 30
.EE
.RE
.PP
Extract the mean idle latency in milliseconds from a scripted run, without
pulling in a JSON parser. The
.B idle
object is the one measured before any transfer; the parallel
.B loaded
object is the one taken while the link is saturated:
.PP
.RS
.EX
cfspeed \-\-ping \-\-json \e
  | grep \-o '"idle":{"avg":[0-9.]*' \e
  | cut \-d: \-f3
.EE
.RE
.PP
Run a test without touching the history file:
.PP
.RS
.EX
cfspeed \-\-no-history
.EE
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR curl (1),
.BR ping (1),
.BR iperf3 (1)
.PP
Cloudflare's public speed test endpoints:
.I https://speed.cloudflare.com
